<h2>The marriage and the allegations</h2>
<p>The parties married in January 2016; a son was born that December. The husband alleged that the wife behaved abnormally from the outset — laughing without reason, going out improperly dressed — and that doctors at Ranchi had diagnosed schizophrenia, an illness he said was concealed before the marriage. The wife denied any illness. Her case was that she had been harassed over a demand of Rs. 5,00,000 and sent away to her parental home in 2021, and that she remained willing to live with her husband. Crucially, a Medical Board at RINPAS, Kanke — a government mental health institution — had examined her and found nothing abnormal.</p>
<p>The Family Court at Chatra dismissed the husband's suit under Section 13(1)(ia) and (iii) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955. He appealed under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</p>

<h2>The evidence, weighed</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Husband's case</th><th>What the record showed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Wife suffers from schizophrenia, diagnosed at Ranchi</td><td>RINPAS Medical Board: she "does not appear to have any psychiatric illness"; Board members never cross-examined</td></tr>
<tr><td>Illness concealed before marriage</td><td>The families knew each other beforehand; concealment not established</td></tr>
<tr><td>His expert supported the diagnosis</td><td>The expert reported only mild abnormality, without a clear diagnosis of schizophrenia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cruelty by abnormal conduct</td><td>No cogent and reliable material proving specific acts of cruelty</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">
<p>The Division Bench reiterated the Supreme Court's standard: a mental disorder justifies dissolution only when it is of a degree that makes it impossible for the petitioner reasonably to live with the respondent. The mere existence of a disorder of any degree is not sufficient in law. Finding no perversity in the Family Court's appraisal, the appeal was dismissed.</p>
</div>

<h2>The structure of a Section 13(1)(iii) case</h2>
<p>Three features of this judgment repay attention. First, the burden never shifts: the spouse who asserts the disorder must prove its existence, its kind and its degree, ordinarily through psychiatric evidence that survives cross-examination. Second, institutional medical opinion carries weight that private assertions cannot match — once a statutory Medical Board reported no illness, the petitioner's case needed confrontation of that report, and the failure even to seek cross-examination of the Board was treated as telling. Third, the cruelty ground cannot ride on the coat-tails of the illness ground: conduct said to flow from a disputed illness must independently satisfy the test of cruelty, with particulars.</p>
<p>Family courts see a steady stream of petitions where distress within a marriage is recast as psychiatric disorder. The judgment is a reminder that courts will neither stigmatise a spouse on loose allegations nor dissolve a marriage on a diagnosis that the evidence does not sustain.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Before pleading mental disorder, secure a genuine clinical foundation: treatment records, prescriptions, hospitalisation papers — not recollections of behaviour.</p>
<p>If an adverse Medical Board report enters the record, apply to cross-examine its members; leaving it unchallenged is usually fatal.</p>
<p>Plead cruelty with particulars — dates, incidents, witnesses — and prove at least the principal instances; generalities will not carry a decree.</p>
<p>Respondents facing such allegations should seek examination by a government institution early; an independent clean report reframes the entire case.</p>
</div>
